# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification", problem 3 d), with its solution sheet.
id: conditional-even
title: Fill in the precondition of a conditional (d)
topic: Conditional
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 3 d
tags: conditional, wp, even, odd

task:
  Fill in the weakest precondition of the following program.

pre: (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (x <= 0 ==> even(z))
post: even(x)

program:
  {R3}
  if (x > 0) then {
    {R1}
    x := x + 1        // then
  } else {
    {R2}
    x := z            // else
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The then branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, Q)
  = even(x)[x <- x + 1]
  = even(x + 1)
  = odd(x)              // x + 1 is even exactly when x is odd
set: R1 = odd(x)

step: The else branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
calc:
  wp(x := z, Q)
  = even(x)[x <- z]
  = even(z)
set: R2 = even(z)

step: Combine the branches
rule: IF, WP
focus: if
calc:
  wp(if (x > 0) then x := x + 1 else x := z, Q)
  = (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (!(x > 0) ==> even(z))
  = (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (x <= 0 ==> even(z))
set: R3 = (x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (x <= 0 ==> even(z))
result: The weakest precondition is $(x > 0 ==> odd(x)) && (x <= 0 ==> even(z))$.

summary:
  The formula reads as a case analysis: if $x$ is positive it must be odd,
  so that adding one makes it even; otherwise $z$ must be even, since its
  value is copied into $x$. It could be rewritten with the distributive
  laws, but the case analysis is the clearest form.
